  "script": {
    "segments": [
      {
        "narration": "Every night, the trees started screaming. For months in 1829, locals near the Fontainebleau forest reported blood-curdling cries echoing from the dense woods after dark.",
        "visual_query": "dense french forest canopy",
        "visual_fallback": "dark moody woods"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Woodcutters dropped their iron axes and fled as invisible voices wept directly from the oak trunks. How could solid timber sound like a terrified human?",
        "visual_query": "old oak tree bark",
        "visual_fallback": "mossy tree trunk"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Botanists and local authorities searched the dark canopy, finding no hidden people, no trapped animals, and no physical damage to explain the noise.",
        "visual_query": "foggy forest path at dusk",
        "visual_fallback": "misty woods"
      },
      {
        "narration": "The terrifying chorus was actually generated by millions of micro-cicadas hidden in the bark, amplified by hollow frost-split timber acting like a giant natural speaker.",
        "visual_query": "macro insect on tree bark",
        "visual_fallback": "forest insects"
      },
      {
        "narration": "When winter frost split the frozen sapwood, the wind turned the entire forest into a massive acoustic instrument. What other sounds are hiding in plain sight?",
        "visual_query": "winter frost on tree branches",
        "visual_fallback": "cold winter trees"
      }
    ],
